00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.570 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in 1990 my mom was having a big year she  was going up for a huge promotion SVP at  Morgan Stanley at the same time she was  going through a more anxiety inducing  process she was trying to get pregnant  now my mom was the only woman in her  product group and she was a go-getter  she was also 31 and my parents were  having some trouble conceiving this  meant that my mom wasn't just trying to  get pregnant she was going through IVF  her 12-hour workday  now featured secret trips to a broom  closet where she had to inject a large  needle full of hormones while her male  colleagues went to client happy hours  she made up excuses and snuck off for  weekly trips to the doctor my mom  covered her tracks because she wasn't  sure what her male colleagues would  think if they found out she was trying  to have a baby as she hid in that closet  she wondered would her boss think she  was less committed to her job would she  be passed over when they staff important  deals ultimately my mom got that  promotion but along the way she  miscarried now my mom didn't share any  of this with her coworkers and it turns  out it's probably good that she didn't  research on bias against pregnant  employees is far-ranging and scarily  conclusive pregnancy bias is real the  work force is 47% women and more and  more of those women are delaying  childbirth to be a part of that  workforce but the workplace isn't  changing to meet that reality women are  still trapped in their own metaphorical  broom closets it's time to stop the  secrecy and open up a dialogue so how  can employers help reduce stigma around  pregnancy and fertility at work and  improve outcomes for all women all  parents  in the process before we answer that  first we have to ask what is pregnancy  bias to do that we're gonna do a little  experiment raise your hands if you plan  on having children whoa way more than I  practiced having it's the answer all  right  so raise your hands again if you plan on  working while you or your partner is  pregnant same answer great now these are  hands again if you're gonna tell your  employer immediately about your  pregnancy nobody cool all right  so men in the room you should tell  everybody research shows us that once  you're a parent you'll be paid more  hired more frequently can be seen is  more responsible but women for us the  story is different for us Parenthood is  a penalty controlling for hours worked  experience industry and education  pregnant women and new mothers will be  hired less paid less and seen as less  competent the New York Times found  startling evidence of this even relative  to their own evaluations pre pregnancy  once pregnant women were immediately  seen as less committed they were left  out of important meetings and not given  the same bonuses and y'all this problem  is only getting worse the Equal  Opportunity Employment Commission found  that this has been rising for over 20  years when they studied pregnancy  discrimination claims and it's actually  at its highest point ever this could be  partially caused by our changing  fertility landscape the average age of  first child birth is also at its highest  ever especially for women with  undergraduate or graduate degrees take  us again as an example the women in this  room will likely have their first child  at age 33 but this trend of later stage  Parenthood means that for us having a  baby might not be as simple some of us  like my mom  need fertility assistance and treatments  like IVF our time and emotion intensive  they can impact the workday and compound  our fears of being seen as less  committed and though male factor  infertility is the root cause of nearly  half of infertility cases for couples  the treatments fall squarely on the  shoulders of women now you understand  the dynamics in play the reasons my mom  hid in that closet  women are caught in this terrible  tension between pursuing their goals of  motherhood and being seen as less  invested to the careers they spent years  building and pregnancy bias isn't just  bad for women it's bad for families and  employers too so if you've been chilling  because you don't have a uterus it's  time to check back in you need to build  this bias into your budget if you plan  on having a dual income household with  children your female partner will  statistically never recover her earning  power she will make on average $16,000  less a year after having her first child  that's a loss of over a million dollars  by the time you're both ready to retire  and as future employers we need to  prepare for the attrition of valuable  employees from the workforce given the  penalty of motherhood and the rising  cost of childcare many women debate the  economics of returning to work so it's  clear what we must do reduce pregnancy  bias and reduce the economic loss of  talented women leaving the workforce but  hundreds of years of gender bias that is  a lot to deal with all at once so let's  start by tackling the stigma around  starting a family a space we can look to  for inspiration is mental health you  know to employers employees dealing with  mental health are not dissimilar from  women trying to get pregnant  in both cases people are dealing with  highly emotional considerations may be  juggling doctor's appointments and may  fear the judgment  of their co-workers many companies has  successfully implemented policies to  reduce stigma around mental health and  specifically there are three that we can  adopt first let's dedicate HR resources  to pregnancy and fertility  let's sponsor educational programs that  clearly lay out fertility policy and  Family Leave  putting our voices strongly behind these  programs demonstrates commitment to  employees trying to become parents and  it creates a culture of support not  stigma second let's dedicate physical  spaces on-site for women dealing with  fertility there's no reason that someone  going through IVF should be injecting  hormones in a closet creating spaces for  this the same way we do for diabetics  injecting insulin helps dispel the sense  of shame and secrecy and it also  minimizes interruption to the work day  it's a win-win third and finally we need  to share stories not just from women but  from everyone particularly from senior  leadership Deloitte hosted a series of  fireside chats with partners who were  dealing with mental health at work and  the results were immediate employees  immediately said that they felt reduced  feelings of stigma and isolation talking  about pregnancy and fertility the same  way not only serves to educate those who  may not be aware of the impact but it  makes us more empathetic colleagues so  sharing stories is exactly why I'm here  today though the first round didn't go  quite as planned my brother and I were  the successful result of IVF cycle  number two so I'm here but unfortunately  my mom isn't we lost her to a battle  with cancer 18 years ago and that's why  I'm on stage in front of you right now  she would have wanted me to share her  story to keep another woman from  fighting these battles alone  so here's my final ask let's be better  future employers let's think about  pregnancy bias when we design our  workspaces or allocate our HR resources  let's think about pregnancy bias when we  hire when we staff when we promote let's  share our stories all of us let's be  more empathetic employers of not only  women but all parents you've listened to  me now go out and listen to them thank  you  [Applause]  [Music]

